Fig. 6 Data Statistics of SCxGen-32M. (A) Overview of the distribution of tissues where cells come from. (B) Overview of the distribution of cell development stage. (C) Overview of the distribution of cell type categories.

SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE

Multi-panel data visualization with three labeled panels: A, B, and C.

Panel A is a pie chart titled “Tissue Source Distribution,” showing tissue categories. The largest slices are Brain and Blood, followed by smaller categories including Others, Breast, Lung, Eye, Heart, Intestine, and a bracketed group of low-frequency tissues such as Kidney, Skin, Airway, Placenta, Bone Marrow, Liver, Lymph Node, and Adrenal Gland.

Panel B is a pie chart showing age or developmental group distribution. Labels include Adult, Middle Aged, Fetus/Embryo, Elderly, Young Adult, Other, and Child/Adolescent. Adult and Middle Aged appear to be the largest groups.

Panel C is a treemap of cell type distribution. Large blocks include oligodendrocyte, CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell, glutamatergic neuron, L2/3-6 intratelencephalic projecting glutamatergic neuron, CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell, classical monocyte, B cell, natural killer cell, fibroblast, macrophage, astrocyte, and other smaller immune, neural, epithelial, stromal, and endothelial cell categories.

Main visible finding: the dataset is dominated by brain and blood tissue sources, adult/middle-aged age groups, and several major neural and immune cell types.
